{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nThe `tickets` pack grows ~2.0 KiB/commit at high daily commit volume because each enrichment write emits its own git commit and re-emits the root tree. Below-horizon collapse (S1) removes historical churn; W1 removes the post-enrich finalize churn at the source so it does not re-accumulate after reclaim lands.\n\n## Scope\n\nBatch the post-enrich finalize events for one successful enrichment item into ONE write/commit: `TICKET_DIGEST` + `DONE_ENRICH` collapse from two commits to one commit (one root-tree re-emission). `CLAIM_ENRICH` remains its own committed and auto-pushed event, visible before the LLM/enrich call, because `_live_drainer_ids` / `_MAX_CONCURRENT_DRAINERS` and `queue.claim()` arbitrate cross-clone drainers by reading committed post-enqueue `CLAIM_ENRICH` events. `ENQUEUE_ENRICH` remains unchanged as an earlier lifecycle event emitted at plan-review certification / stale-digest self-heal.\n\nPreserve observable ticket state, event semantics, per-event signing/verifiability, and optimistic-concurrency guarantees. Batch the git commit only; each event remains its own event file with its own authorship material.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nThe history reclaim of already-accumulated commits (that is S1). No change to the event schema's observable contract beyond commit batching. No change to queue claim arbitration, drainer concurrency caps, enqueue timing, or cross-clone visibility of `CLAIM_ENRICH`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The post-enrich finalize events (`TICKET_DIGEST` + `DONE_ENRICH`) for a single successful enrichment item collapse into ONE commit (one root-tree re-emission), measured on a fixture; `CLAIM_ENRICH` remains a separate committed event for cross-clone drainer arbitration.\n- [ ] Observable reduced ticket state is unchanged vs the pre-batch behavior (reducer-equality/public-reader fixture passes).\n- [ ] Each batched event retains its individual signature and passes `rebar verify-authorship`.\n- [ ] Optimistic-concurrency / audit guarantees are preserved (existing drainer arbitration and concurrency tests pass; `CLAIM_ENRICH` stays visible before enrichment).\n- [ ] The measured commits-per-enrich-burst and per-burst root-tree re-emission count drop from CLAIM + DIGEST + DONE (3 commits) to CLAIM + batched-finalize (2 commits), while `ENQUEUE_ENRICH` remains unchanged as a separate earlier lifecycle event.\n\n## Portability\n\nPure library/store change; no dependence on any single CI provider.\n\n## Implementation plan (2026-09-01, narrowed by operator decision; revised for partial-failure contract)\n\n### Worked example\n\nFor an already-soaked queued ticket, the drain currently emits `CLAIM_ENRICH`, then after the LLM returns emits `TICKET_DIGEST`, then `DONE_ENRICH`, each as its own committed ticket-store write. W1 keeps the first write exactly as-is: `CLAIM_ENRICH {ticket_id, drainer_id, lease_expires_ns}` remains committed and pushed before enrichment so other clones can observe the live claim and avoid duplicate spend. The only normal-path change is the finalize lane after a successful enrich: `TICKET_DIGEST {schema, digest, content_hash, model, digest_hash_version}` and `DONE_ENRICH {ticket_id}` are composed as separate signed events but flushed through one batch commit.\n\nConcrete partial-failure example: if digest event composition/signing is refused and `digest_sidecar.emit()` returns `False`, `queue.mark_done()` is still called exactly as the current code does, so permanent retry/terminal semantics are not strengthened or weakened by batching. The batch buffer then contains whatever the existing helper calls successfully composed (DONE only in that example) and flushes that. If the batch commit itself fails after both events were composed, the helper logs the batch failure and falls back to the existing sequential direct calls in the same order (`digest_sidecar.emit()` then `queue.mark_done()` outside the sink). That fallback preserves the previous best-effort failure posture; the accepted optimization is the normal successful path, not a new durability contract under store faults.\n\n### Mechanism\n\n1. In `rebar.llm.enrich_drain._finalize_claims`, replace the success path's two independent normal writes (`digest_sidecar.emit(...)` then `queue.mark_done(...)`) with an enrichment-local helper that opens `_commands._seam.batch_sink`, calls those same append helpers in the same order and with the same ignored return-value contract as today, then flushes the buffered events with `_store.event_append.batch_write_and_push()`.\n2. The helper preserves the helpers' current best-effort semantics: composition/signing failures are swallowed by the existing helpers; the following helper call still runs as before; and a zero-length buffer returns without a commit. Batch commit failures degrade to the old sequential direct write path after rollback, with a warning, because batching must not introduce a new false terminal/failure posture.\n3. Do not change `queue.enqueue`, `queue.claim`, `queue.mark_done`, `digest_sidecar.emit`, event schema, `_live_drainer_ids`, `_MAX_CONCURRENT_DRAINERS`, or queue reducers. The public event stream after a successful finalize still contains the same `TICKET_DIGEST` and `DONE_ENRICH` events in order; only their wrapping git commit count changes.\n4. Leave permanent-rejection tombstones, stale-writeback re-enqueue behavior, and `CLAIM_ENRICH` arbitration unchanged. CLAIM remains outside the batch by design and visible before enrichment.\n\n### Alternatives considered\n\nA. Batch only the post-enrich finalize pair: chosen by the operator on 2026-09-01 because it reduces source churn without changing cross-clone drainer arbitration. B. Batch CLAIM with finalize using local reservations: rejected because committed CLAIM events are the cross-clone coordination contract. C. Hold the drain advisory lock across the LLM to avoid committed CLAIM: rejected because existing concurrency work requires enrichment outside the advisory lock. D. Make the finalize pair strictly atomic with no fallback: rejected because W1 should batch commit count, not change best-effort behavior under store faults.\n\n### Test plan / RED-first oracle\n\n- Add a fixture test in `tests/unit/test_enrich_drain.py` that enqueues a ticket, records the tracker commit count, runs one drain, and asserts exactly two new commits: one containing `CLAIM_ENRICH`, and one containing both `TICKET_DIGEST` and `DONE_ENRICH`.\n- The same test asserts public postconditions: digest is present/fresh, queue state is `done == True` and `pending == False`, and sidecar events do not alter schema-visible ticket state.\n- Add `rebar verify-authorship --require-authenticated` or the fixture-appropriate authorship verification command after the batched finalize to prove each batched event retains individual signing/verifiability.\n- Run existing arbitration/concurrency tests, especially `test_concurrent_drains_make_zero_duplicate_llm_calls`, to prove `CLAIM_ENRICH` visibility and optimistic-concurrency behavior are unchanged.\n- Add a targeted partial-failure unit test by monkeypatching the batch flush to raise after buffered composition, asserting the old direct helper calls are invoked in order as fallback rather than changing drain failure semantics.\n- Preflight with targeted pytest first, then `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and the project's test gate before push.\n\n### Surface-parity census\n\nCLI command: in scope through `rebar enrich --drain`/`cmd_enrich` because it routes to `enrich_drain.drain`. MCP/API: no separate schema change; enrichment callers share the same library drain core, accepted residual gap unless a direct MCP enrich surface appears. Library facade: in scope through direct `enrich_drain.drain`, `queue`, and `digest_sidecar` fixture tests. Automated callers: in scope because `_store.event_append.write_and_push -> _maybe_enrich_drain -> maybe_drain -> drain` remains the automated path, with CLAIM still committed before enrichment. Read/dual direction: in scope through `latest_ticket_digest`, `freshness`, `queue.reduce_ticket`, and `pending_enrichment`; reducer-visible ticket state stays unchanged.\n\n### Acceptance mapping\n\nAC1/AC5: commit-count/root-tree tests showing CLAIM remains separate while finalize pair is one commit. AC2: public reducer/reader postconditions. AC3: authorship verification on the fixture. AC4: unchanged public queue contracts plus existing drain concurrency tests and fallback-order coverage.\n"}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788283086699150542,"uuid":"3b42b62f-4573-4be4-a872-3cb2fcf4d919"}